# The ukulele first, as school programmes and the children's courses start: four nylon strings,
# small enough for a young hand, and tuned so the open strings already make a chord. The picture is
# upright, facing the child, the same way round as every chord card they will read.
lesson music-uke-meet v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=3 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Meet the ukulele"
  goal "Name the parts of a ukulele and its four strings, G, C, E and A, and number them from the thinnest."

  look {
    scene 34x29 {
      ukulele uke labels=true letters=true ring=0 at=canvas(1, 1)
      chordbox card tuning=uke chord=none places=[] frets=3 show=dots name=false letters=true lit=[] right-of=uke gap=1
    }
    say """
      A ukulele has four strings: G, C, E and A. Stand it up with the strings facing you and they go
      G, C, E, A from left to right, the same as on a chord card. String 1 is A, the thinnest.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.uke-string-letter
    practice music.uke-which-string count=2 seed=3
    show music.uke-string-number
    level easy {
      worked music.uke-number-of L="G" level=medium
    }
    practice music.uke-number-of count=2 seed=6
    practice music.uke-parts-match count=1 hard-count=0 seed=2
    level hard {
      practice music.uke-string-claim count=1 seed=7
    }
    show music.uke-play-open
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.uke-string-claim k=2 easy-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.uke-low-to-high
  }
  remember {
    say "Four strings, G C E A. String 1 is A, the thinnest one."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Tune the ukulele for the child with a clip-on tuner before each practice; new strings stretch
    and drift for the first week. Show them how to hold it, body against the tummy and the neck to
    the left, then stand it up beside the card so they can see the strings match.

    The two-star question names the usual slip, counting the strings from the left. In the
    three-star question the G string is tuned high, so the lowest sound is the C string and the
    order is C, E, G, A; let the child find that by listening before you say so.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the ringed string is the A at the edge or the E beside it, the G string's number is found once in the teacher's pen before the C and E strings, the parts and the strings to play are as written, Nia's claim is about string 1, at the edge, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the ringed string is one of the two in the middle, the numbers to find are the G and C strings, the parts make way for a second claim of Nia's, the strings to play are as written, Nia's claim is about string 2, in the middle, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
